import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CDU0sSpB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects","description":"How Hearth makes sends, writes, deletes, publishes, and other real-world actions previewable, authorized, reconcilable, and recoverable.","frontmatter":{"title":"Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects","description":"How Hearth makes sends, writes, deletes, publishes, and other real-world actions previewable, authorized, reconcilable, and recoverable.","outline":false},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/design/effects.md","filePath":"en/design/effects.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"en/design/effects.md"};function i(o,e,d,l,c,p){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="approvals-takeover-and-external-effects" tabindex="-1">Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects <a class="header-anchor" href="#approvals-takeover-and-external-effects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects&quot;">​</a></h1><p class="lead">The real risk is not that Hearth writes text. It is that Hearth sends email, changes files, submits forms, deletes content, pays, or publishes on the user&#39;s behalf. External actions need a clear preview, authorization, execution, and receipt; a model saying &quot;finished&quot; is not evidence that the result happened.</p><h2 id="design-goals" tabindex="-1">Design Goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-goals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design Goals&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before an action happens, the user must see exactly what it will do: the target system, target object, key parameters, likely impact, reversibility, and required authority. Approval applies to that specific action, not a vague &quot;let the assistant use my mailbox&quot;. Parameters changing, scope expanding, or approval expiring requires confirmation again.</p><p>Every external action needs a verifiable result. Before execution the system records the pending intent; afterward it stores a receipt with time, target, result, and reversal method. If the network breaks or a service returns an uncertain result, Hearth reconciles before resending. A confirmed failure enters a queue that can be reviewed, repaired, and replayed.</p><p>Human takeover is part of the design, not a last resort. A user can pause a running task, keep current progress and completed actions, operate directly, and return the result for verification. Key actions and judgments during takeover are recorded so later improvements have evidence.</p><p>Approval is graded by risk and reversibility. Reading and organizing usually need limited authority. Sending, publishing, deleting, paying, and operating devices need stronger confirmation. Irreversible or high-value actions stay bounded by amount, target, or time even when pre-authorized.</p><h2 id="design-tradeoffs" tabindex="-1">Design Tradeoffs <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-tradeoffs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design Tradeoffs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>External actions follow a sequence: preview, authorize, execute, then reconcile. The system and its executors cannot bypass this path to call an external service directly. Connectors and desktop executors only complete actions inside their authorization; they do not decide whether the action should happen.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action type</th><th>Default policy</th><th>Exception</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Read, search, organize</td><td>Authorize within the task scope</td><td>Narrow scope and warn when sensitive data is involved</td></tr><tr><td>Create drafts or files</td><td>Execute directly, retaining artifacts and versions</td><td>Preview differences before overwriting an original</td></tr><tr><td>Send messages or submit forms</td><td>Confirm target, content, and scope each time</td><td>Low-risk repetitions may be pre-authorized inside a clear scope</td></tr><tr><td>Delete, publish, pay, or operate a device</td><td>Human confirmation by default</td><td>Never unconditional automatic execution</td></tr><tr><td>Actions that cannot be made reversible</td><td>Human takeover or explicit risk acceptance</td><td>Do not bypass through &quot;trying a few more times&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The authorization scope must be understandable to an ordinary user. An approval card explains &quot;who, what, when, and how far&quot; in natural language while preserving expandable technical details for verification. After a rejection, the system cannot rephrase and ask for the same action again.</p><p>Idempotency and reconciliation are the foundation of external actions. One send creates one message; one submission creates one record; retries reuse the same identity. When an external system returns no clear result, Hearth enters an uncertain state and queries or asks the user instead of executing again.</p><p>Human error is also input. A user may approve the wrong content, undo a correct action, or give conflicting instructions. The system keeps context and supports correction, compensation, and re-approval. The goal is a clear responsibility boundary, not pretending the system never makes mistakes.</p>',13)])])}const f=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
